ImportantPourquoi ce chapitre est central pour un ingénieur



Dans un contexte d’ingénierie, on n’observe presque jamais toute la population :

on mesure quelques pièces pour contrôler une production ;
on teste quelques composants pour évaluer une fiabilité ;
on relève quelques capteurs pour estimer une grandeur physique ;
on inspecte un lot pour estimer un taux de défauts.

La question n’est donc pas seulement “quelle valeur a-t-on observée ?”, mais “de combien cette valeur peut-elle fluctuer d’un échantillon à l’autre ?”. La distribution d’échantillonnage répond précisément à cette question et fournit la base mathématique des intervalles de confiance, des tests et des règles de décision.

Interactive Exploration
Use the controls below to explore how each function family behaves as you vary its parameters.

viewof funcType = Inputs.select(
  ["affine", "quadratic", "exponential", "logarithmic"],
  {value: "affine", label: "Function type"}
)

viewof paramA = Inputs.range([-5, 5], {
  value: 1,
  step: 0.5,
  label: "Parameter a"
})

viewof paramB = Inputs.range([-10, 10], {
  value: 0,
  step: 0.5,
  label: "Parameter b"
})























xDomain = Array.from({length: 200}, (_, i) =&gt; -5 + i * 0.05)

funcData = xDomain.flatMap(x =&gt; {
  let y
  if (funcType === "affine") y = paramA * x + paramB
  else if (funcType === "quadratic") y = paramA * x * x + paramB
  else if (funcType === "exponential") y = Math.exp(paramA * x) + paramB
  else if (funcType === "logarithmic") {
    if (x &lt;= 0) return []
    y = paramA * Math.log(x) + paramB
  }
  if (y === undefined || !isFinite(y) || Math.abs(y) &gt; 30) return []
  return [{x, y}]
})

funcChart = Plot.plot({
  marks: [
    Plot.line(funcData, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "steelblue"}),
    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "#aaa", strokeDash: [2,2]}),
    Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: "#aaa", strokeDash: [2,2]})
  ],
  x: {domain: [-5, 5], label: "x"},
  y: {domain: [-15, 15], label: "f(x)"},
  width: 500,
  height: 320
})

domainText = funcType === "logarithmic"
  ? "Domain: ]0, +∞[ (x must be strictly positive)"
  : "Domain: ℝ (all real numbers)"






























html`
&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1rem"&gt;${funcChart}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="metric-grid"&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Function type&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${funcType}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Domain&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${domainText}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
`







Investigation

Select logarithmic. What happens when you try to evaluate at \(x = 0\) or \(x &lt; 0\)? Why does the graph start at \(x &gt; 0\)?
Switch to quadratic and set \(a &gt; 0\), then \(a &lt; 0\). How does the sign of \(a\) affect the graph shape and the location of the maximum or minimum?
Which function type best models the “rapid growth then plateau” pattern from the Opening Problem? Set the parameters to demonstrate your answer.

Interactive Exploration
Use the sliders below to explore how arithmetic and geometric sequences compare as you vary the initial term, common difference, and common ratio.

viewof u0 = Inputs.range([0, 100], {
  value: 28,
  step: 1,
  label: "Initial term u₀"
})

viewof d = Inputs.range([-10, 10], {
  value: 1.5,
  step: 0.5,
  label: "Common difference d (arithmetic)"
})

viewof r = Inputs.range([0.5, 2.0], {
  value: 1.05,
  step: 0.01,
  label: "Common ratio r (geometric)"
})

viewof nTerms = Inputs.range([2, 20], {
  value: 10,
  step: 1,
  label: "Number of terms n"
})






























indices = Array.from({length: nTerms}, (_, i) =&gt; i)
arithmeticSeq = indices.map(n =&gt; ({n, value: u0 + n * d, type: "Arithmetic"}))
geometricSeq = indices.map(n =&gt; ({n, value: u0 * Math.pow(r, n), type: "Geometric"}))
allData = [...arithmeticSeq, ...geometricSeq]

seqChart = Plot.plot({
  marks: [
    Plot.line(allData, {x: "n", y: "value", stroke: "type"}),
    Plot.dot(allData, {x: "n", y: "value", fill: "type"})
  ],
  x: {label: "Term index n"},
  y: {label: "Value"},
  color: {legend: true}
})





































html`
&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1rem"&gt;${seqChart}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="metric-grid"&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Arithmetic u₍ₙ₎ (last term)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${(u0 + (nTerms-1)*d).toFixed(2)}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Geometric u₍ₙ₎ (last term)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${(u0 * Math.pow(r, nTerms-1)).toFixed(2)}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
`







Investigation

Set \(u_0 = 28\), \(d = 1.5\), \(r = 1.05\), \(n = 10\). Which sequence is larger at the last term?
Increase \(n\) to 20. At what point does the geometric sequence overtake the arithmetic one?
Set \(r = 0.88\) and \(d = -4\). Which model represents faster depreciation?

Interactive Exploration
Use the sliders below to explore how fixed cost, variable cost, and selling price interact to determine the break-even quantity.

viewof fixedCost = Inputs.range([0, 5000], {
  value: 2000,
  step: 100,
  label: "Fixed cost F (€)"
})

viewof variableCost = Inputs.range([0, 100], {
  value: 3,
  step: 0.5,
  label: "Variable cost per unit v (€)"
})

viewof price = Inputs.range([0, 200], {
  value: 8,
  step: 0.5,
  label: "Selling price per unit p (€)"
})























margin = price - variableCost
bep = margin &gt; 0 ? fixedCost / margin : Infinity
revenueAtBep = price * bep
costAtBep = fixedCost + variableCost * bep
profitAtBep = revenueAtBep - costAtBep





































html`
&lt;div class="metric-grid"&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Contribution margin (p − v)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${margin.toFixed(2)} €&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Break-even quantity Q*&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${margin &gt; 0 ? bep.toFixed(0) + " units" : "No break-even (p ≤ v)"}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Revenue at Q*&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${margin &gt; 0 ? revenueAtBep.toFixed(2) + " €" : "—"}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric-card"&gt;
    &lt;span class="metric-label"&gt;Total cost at Q*&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;${margin &gt; 0 ? costAtBep.toFixed(2) + " €" : "—"}&lt;/strong&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
`







Investigation

Set the selling price equal to the variable cost. What happens to the break-even quantity? Why?
Double the fixed cost. How does \(Q^*\) change? Is the relationship linear?
A competitor offers the same product at €6. What break-even quantity would your café need at this price?

This course will help you develop the mathematical reasoning and calculation skills needed to solve quantitative problems in finance, economics, and management.
Throughout the course, you will learn to:

Perform calculations accurately;
Translate practical problems into mathematical expressions;
Choose an appropriate calculation method;
Interpret equations, functions, graphs, and numerical results;
Use mathematical tools to support managerial decisions.

